<% content_for :title, "Triggers, Bipolar & Addiction | Bipolar Sober" %>

<main class="addiction-triggers-page">

  <!-- HERO -->
  <section class="addiction-triggers-hero">
    <div class="addiction-triggers-hero-content">

      <p class="addiction-triggers-eyebrow">
        BIPOLAR SOBER / ADDICTION / TRIGGERS
      </p>

      <h1>
        <span>KNOW WHAT</span>
        <span>LIGHTS THE</span>
        <span>FUSE.</span>
      </h1>

      <p class="addiction-triggers-intro">
        THE URGE DOESN'T ALWAYS<br>
        COME OUT OF FUCKING NOWHERE.
      </p>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 01 -->
  <section class="addiction-triggers-section at-pink">

    <div class="at-heading">
      <p class="at-number">01 / TRIGGERS</p>

      <h2>
        SOMETHING<br>
        HAPPENS.<br>
        THEN WHAT?
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="at-copy">

      <p class="at-lead">
        A trigger is something that sets off thoughts, emotions,
        memories, cravings, or urges connected to using.
      </p>

      <p>
        Sometimes it's obvious: a bar, a bottle, a person,
        a place, a shitty day.
      </p>

      <p>
        Sometimes it's happening entirely inside you:
        pain, depression, racing thoughts, anger, loneliness,
        exhaustion, or a mood shift.
      </p>

      <p>
        Knowing the trigger doesn't magically make the craving disappear.
      </p>

      <p>
        It gives you a chance to recognize what the fuck is happening
        before the trigger starts making decisions for you.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        A TRIGGER IS INFORMATION.<br>
        NOT AN INSTRUCTION.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 02 -->
  <section class="addiction-triggers-section at-yellow">

    <div class="at-heading">
      <p class="at-number">02 / MY BIG THREE</p>

      <h2>
        PAIN.<br>
        MANIA.<br>
        DEPRESSION.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="at-copy">

      <p class="at-lead">
        My triggers aren't just bottles sitting on a shelf.
      </p>

      <p>
        Some of the biggest ones start inside my own body and brain.
      </p>

      <div class="at-big-three">

        <div>
          <span>SCIATICA</span>
          <p>
            Physical pain. Frustration. Being limited.
            Wanting relief. Wanting to feel something other
            than what my body is making me feel.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>MANIA</span>
          <p>
            Energy. Impulsivity. Confidence. Risk-taking.
            Fuck-it thinking. Feeling unstoppable and wanting
            to keep the intensity going.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>DEPRESSION</span>
          <p>
            Emptiness. Exhaustion. Hopelessness. Isolation.
            Wanting numbness, escape, relief, or simply
            wanting to feel different.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

      <blockquote>
        THREE DIFFERENT STATES.<br>
        SAME DANGEROUS QUESTION:<br>
        "HOW DO I MAKE THIS FEEL DIFFERENT?"
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 03 -->
  <section class="addiction-triggers-section at-black">

    <div class="at-heading">
      <p class="at-number">03 / SCIATICA</p>

      <h2>
        WHEN<br>
        YOUR BODY<br>
        WON'T SHUT UP.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="at-copy">

      <p class="at-lead">
        Pain can wear down your ability to deal with everything else.
      </p>

      <p>
        It's not just the physical sensation.
      </p>

      <p>
        It's being frustrated with your body.
        It's not being able to do what you want.
        It's shitty sleep.
        It's boredom.
        It's irritability.
        It's wanting a break from being aware of the pain.
      </p>

      <p>
        That's where addiction can offer a very convincing sales pitch:
      </p>

      <div class="at-trigger-thought">
        JUST CHANGE HOW YOU FEEL.
      </div>

      <p>
        The craving may not actually be saying,
        <strong>"I want alcohol."</strong>
      </p>

      <p>
        It may be saying,
        <strong>"I don't want to feel like this anymore."</strong>
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        SOMETIMES THE TRIGGER ISN'T THE SUBSTANCE.<br>
        IT'S WANTING OUT OF YOUR BODY FOR A WHILE.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 04 -->
  <section class="addiction-triggers-section at-purple">

    <div class="at-heading">
      <p class="at-number">04 / MANIA</p>

      <h2>
        EVERYTHING<br>
        SOUNDS LIKE<br>
        A GREAT IDEA.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="at-copy">

      <p class="at-lead">
        Mania can change the way risk, reward,
        confidence, and consequences feel.
      </p>

      <p>
        Suddenly the rules seem negotiable.
      </p>

      <p>
        Drinking sounds fun.
        Spending money sounds reasonable.
        Getting high sounds interesting.
        Something reckless sounds fucking brilliant.
      </p>

      <p>
        And because you may feel good, the danger can be harder
        to recognize than when you're obviously miserable.
      </p>

      <div class="at-warning-grid">

        <div>
          <span>SLEEP</span>
          <p>Am I sleeping less?</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>SPEED</span>
          <p>Are my thoughts, speech, or plans speeding up?</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>SPENDING</span>
          <p>Am I suddenly spending more or caring less about money?</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>RISK</span>
          <p>Are things suddenly feeling safer than they actually are?</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>CONFIDENCE</span>
          <p>Do I feel unusually powerful, certain, or invincible?</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>SUBSTANCES</span>
          <p>Am I suddenly romanticizing drinking or getting high?</p>
        </div>

      </div>

      <blockquote>
        SOMETIMES THE CRAVING IS ALSO<br>
        A FUCKING MOOD WARNING.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 05 -->
  <section class="addiction-triggers-section at-pink">

    <div class="at-heading">
      <p class="at-number">05 / DEPRESSION</p>

      <h2>
        FUCK IT.<br>
        NOTHING<br>
        MATTERS.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="at-copy">

      <p class="at-lead">
        Depression can trigger addiction from the opposite direction.
      </p>

      <p>
        Mania can say:
        <strong>"This will be fucking awesome."</strong>
      </p>

      <p>
        Depression can say:
        <strong>"Who fucking cares?"</strong>
      </p>

      <p>
        Drinking or getting high can start looking like relief
        from emptiness, sadness, exhaustion, isolation,
        boredom, or relentless negative thinking.
      </p>

      <p>
        And when the future feels distant or meaningless,
        consequences can lose some of their power.
      </p>

      <div class="at-trigger-thought">
        IF NOTHING MATTERS, WHY NOT?
      </div>

      <p>
        That's exactly when the thought deserves to be questioned.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        "I DON'T CARE RIGHT NOW"<br>
        DOESN'T MEAN FUTURE-ME WON'T CARE.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 06 -->
  <section class="addiction-triggers-section at-yellow">

    <div class="at-heading">
      <p class="at-number">06 / THE CHAIN</p>

      <h2>
        FIND THE<br>
        FIRST<br>
        DOMINO.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="at-copy">

      <p class="at-lead">
        The craving may be the loudest part of the chain,
        but it isn't necessarily where the chain started.
      </p>

      <div class="at-chain">

        <span>TRIGGER</span>
        <b>→</b>

        <span>THOUGHT</span>
        <b>→</b>

        <span>EMOTION</span>
        <b>→</b>

        <span>URGE</span>
        <b>→</b>

        <span>ACTION</span>
        <b>→</b>

        <span>CONSEQUENCE</span>

      </div>

      <p>
        Maybe it starts with pain.
      </p>

      <p>
        Maybe it starts with three nights of shitty sleep.
      </p>

      <p>
        Maybe it starts with withdrawing from everyone
        because depression is creeping in.
      </p>

      <p>
        The earlier you recognize the chain,
        the more places you have to interrupt it.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        DON'T JUST STUDY THE EXPLOSION.<br>
        FIND THE FUCKING FUSE.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 07 -->
  <section class="addiction-triggers-section at-purple">

    <div class="at-heading">
      <p class="at-number">07 / INTERNAL VS. EXTERNAL</p>

      <h2>
        INSIDE.<br>
        OUTSIDE.<br>
        BOTH.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="at-copy">

      <p class="at-lead">
        Not every trigger lives in the same place.
      </p>

      <div class="at-two-column">

        <div>
          <span>INTERNAL</span>

          <p>Pain</p>
          <p>Mania</p>
          <p>Depression</p>
          <p>Anger</p>
          <p>Loneliness</p>
          <p>Boredom</p>
          <p>Anxiety</p>
          <p>Exhaustion</p>
          <p>Shame</p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>EXTERNAL</span>

          <p>Alcohol</p>
          <p>Stores</p>
          <p>People</p>
          <p>Parties</p>
          <p>Money</p>
          <p>Arguments</p>
          <p>Certain places</p>
          <p>Social situations</p>
          <p>Old routines</p>
        </div>

      </div>

      <p>
        And sometimes an external trigger hits an internal one.
      </p>

      <p>
        You're already depressed, then something shitty happens.
      </p>

      <p>
        You're already manic, then an opportunity for impulsive behavior
        appears right in front of you.
      </p>

      <p>
        You're already hurting, then alcohol becomes available.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        TRIGGERS STACK.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 08 -->
  <section class="addiction-triggers-section at-black">

    <div class="at-heading">
      <p class="at-number">08 / MAP IT</p>

      <h2>
        KNOW YOUR<br>
        OWN FUCKING<br>
        PATTERN.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="at-copy">

      <p class="at-lead">
        Generic trigger lists are useful.
        Your own history is better.
      </p>

      <div class="at-map">

        <div>
          <span>WHAT HAPPENED?</span>
          <p>
            What was going on immediately before the urge?
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>WHAT WAS I FEELING?</span>
          <p>
            Emotionally and physically.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>WHAT WAS MY MOOD DOING?</span>
          <p>
            Sleep, energy, depression, mania, irritability, racing thoughts.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>WHAT DID MY BRAIN SAY?</span>
          <p>
            What thought turned the trigger into permission?
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>WHAT DID I WANT?</span>
          <p>
            Relief? Escape? Excitement? Numbness? Reward?
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>WHAT DID I DO NEXT?</span>
          <p>
            Where was the first place I could have changed direction?
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- 09 -->
  <section class="addiction-triggers-section at-pink">

    <div class="at-heading">
      <p class="at-number">09 / INTERRUPT IT</p>

      <h2>
        SEE IT.<br>
        NAME IT.<br>
        MOVE.
      </h2>
    </div>

    <div class="at-copy">

      <p class="at-lead">
        Recognizing a trigger is only useful if you do something
        with the information.
      </p>

      <div class="at-steps">

        <div>
          <span>01</span>
          <p>
            <strong>NAME THE TRIGGER.</strong><br>
            Pain is setting me off.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>02</span>
          <p>
            <strong>NAME THE URGE.</strong><br>
            I want to change how I feel.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>03</span>
          <p>
            <strong>CHECK THE MOOD.</strong><br>
            Is mania or depression showing up too?
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>04</span>
          <p>
            <strong>CHANGE THE ENVIRONMENT.</strong><br>
            Get away from the opportunity to act.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>05</span>
          <p>
            <strong>GET ANOTHER HUMAN INVOLVED.</strong><br>
            Say what is happening out loud.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div>
          <span>06</span>
          <p>
            <strong>DO SOMETHING FOR THE ACTUAL PROBLEM.</strong><br>
            Respond to the pain, mood, exhaustion, isolation,
            or whatever started the chain.
          </p>
        </div>

      </div>

      <blockquote>
        DON'T JUST FIGHT THE CRAVING.<br>
        RESPOND TO WHAT STARTED IT.
      </blockquote>

    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- ENDING -->
  <section class="addiction-triggers-ending">

    <div>

      <p class="at-ending-small">
        THE FUSE CAN LIGHT.
      </p>

      <h2>
        YOU DON'T<br>
        HAVE TO LET<br>
        <span>IT BURN.</span>
      </h2>

    </div>

    <div class="at-ending-copy">

      <p>
        Pain can show up.
      </p>

      <p>
        Mania can show up.
      </p>

      <p>
        Depression can show up.
      </p>

      <p>
        Cravings can show up.
      </p>

      <p>
        None of them automatically decides what happens next.
      </p>

      <p>
        Learn the pattern early enough and you give yourself
        more places to change direction.
      </p>

      <%= link_to "← BACK TO ADDICTION",
          addiction_path,
          class: "at-back-button" %>

    </div>

  </section>

</main>